WEBVTT

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:13.500
 Are you trying to solve one problem, like hunger in the community, or are you trying to develop the capacity of your community to be able to respond to that problem?

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:30.120
 Something that I think is the anchor of Bounty is that we're trying to push beyond emergency food as a primary response to food insecurity and its close first cousin, food-related illnesses such as obesity.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:42.100
 And so, we're trying to have a community conversation about the shortfalls of our food system and design solutions from the grassroots up that address some of those problems.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:49.060
 It starts with getting people excited to learn where their food comes from and to kickstart their imagination and relationship to food.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:59.840
 And then taking on conversations about why organic costs more, why it should cost more, and what we're doing together to make that still work for everyone.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:09.070
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place.

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:10.070
 I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:16.410
 This season, we're talking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:19.670
 These people have shaped the food movement in California.

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:30.190
 We talk with a diverse group of California's rebel food makers about the ways they do things in their farms, kitchens, and communities that reshape the way we think about food.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.950
 This show is made by Devin Sampson and Chelsea Wills.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:39.530
 Special thanks to the support from Cal Humanities, Food First, and Rebecca Murillo for making this season possible.

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:46.030
 Susie Grady is the director of Petaluma Bounty, an innovative food access program in Sonoma County, California.

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:59.070
 Recognizing that most small-scale farmers can't afford to feed low-income people, and that most low-income consumers can't afford to buy locally-grown produce, Petaluma Bounty works on designing community solutions to hunger.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:10.690
 They connect people to many food assistance programs and run a sliding-scale CSA, a community farm, education programs, and a gleaning program that has distributed over a half million pounds of food.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:15.410
 Hey, welcome, Susie. Welcome to Delicious Revolution.

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:16.930
 Thank you for having me.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:19.250
 We're sitting here at Bounty Farm.

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:21.220
 Yeah, it's beautiful.

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:21.960
 It's the winter.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:25.300
 There's a lot of cover crops, and it just showed me around.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:32.140
 I think I just want to start with the story of how you ended up here at Petaluma Bounty.

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:33.100
 Sure.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:52.430
 So my story is I grew up around farms and did horse training and worked in the hay harvests doing that in Michigan and had a compost research station as a little kid in my garden and just was very much involved in the soil.

00:02:52.430 --> 00:03:07.690
 I lived about five minutes away from the local land grant university, Michigan State University, but I left and went to Ann Arbor, University of Michigan, and studied Latin American politics and political science.

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:16.410
 And there was some political action falling out with our two-party system, et cetera, like most folks.

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:30.080
 And to make a long story longer, I moved out to San Francisco, hoping to join up with an organization that was affecting change around economic system and community development.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:44.770
 Couldn't get a job in that, and so I ended up going and working in law firms, and I got very bored with the urban jungle, the concrete jungle of San Francisco.

00:03:44.770 --> 00:04:00.090
 And so I started doing classes at the Garden for the Environment, and then I became a garden educator and always engaging our community or the community I was working with

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:10.180
 to talk about sustainability and environmental justice, and then went on to UC Santa Cruz and did that apprenticeship program.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:14.370
 And then I came here in 2010 as an intern and worked on the farm.

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:15.150
 At this very farm?

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:15.870
 At this very farm.

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:16.590
 Was it just starting?

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:20.140
 It was the third year of production.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:25.800
 You now are the director of the organization of Petaluma Bounty.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:32.210
 What was Petaluma Bounty when you got here, and what was kind of the vision of it then,

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:33.030
 and how has that changed?

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:33.790
 Sure.

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:34.670
 Great question.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:38.250
 So I'll start kind of with the history of Petaluma Bounty.

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:41.710
 It started with a community needs assessment.

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:53.250
 From 2004 through 2006, local community members, nonprofits, came together to figure out what the greatest unmet need was in Petaluma.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:05:04.680
 And after two years of doing interviews and studies, they figured that it was access to fresh fruits and vegetables, particularly for seniors and children.

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:10.790
 And so Petaluma Bounty came out of that community needs assessment.

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:14.900
 And the first initiative was school gardens and community gardens.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:23.480
 And so fast forward 10 years, we've helped build eight community gardens since then.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:35.250
 And that program is less active now, speaking to how the programs have evolved, because we found that there was enough expertise in the community.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:37.670
 Our help wasn't needed as much.

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:45.180
 The next program was food recovery, a gleaning program, and it still exists today, Bounty Hunters Program.

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:56.630
 It supplements emergency food outlets by going and collecting food from local farms, farmers markets, going out and harvesting food, and taking it to local pantry sites.

00:05:57.030 --> 00:06:00.570
 And after that came the Bounty Farm in 2008.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:02.960
 And this is where we're at today.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:08.960
 And there's a number of different initiatives now than what was started before.

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:17.410
 And some of the initiatives came about because opportunistic funding, right?

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:29.840
 We had a mobile market back in 2008 through 2010, where we'd go to low-income housing sites and schools with a high rate of free and reduced lunches.

00:06:29.950 --> 00:06:33.970
 At that time, we found that it didn't support itself.

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:42.070
 So after we went through the grant funding, we had to stop the program because it was costing more than we could put into it to keep it going.

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:46.530
 And so there's a lot of trial and error.

00:06:48.290 --> 00:07:01.430
 But another initiative that we have held on to is a sliding scale or two-tier price structure for our farm stand that came out of the mobile market, as well as our CSA program.

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:07.610
 And that continues to have mixed levels of success depending on where we're at.

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:16.210
 We're getting more intelligent or experienced and informed about best places to have outlets for fresh fruits and vegetables.

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:27.880
 And trying not to reinvent the wheel as it comes to getting people to go out of their way to get yet another service when most people are so busy.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:44.300
 And so in 2010 and 2011, that's something I really focused on was aligning food access outlets with nutrition education programs and initiatives that are trying to help people make healthy behavior changes.

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:54.320
 Oftentimes, those things are separate and community members have to go to different sides of the city in order to get one service or another.

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:57.040
 And so that's why we moved.

00:07:58.070 --> 00:08:00.330
 We call it our pharmacy with an F.

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:10.870
 Our farm stand, sliding scale farm stand, over to the Petaluma Health Center, which is a federally qualified health center that serves the majority of low-income patients.

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:23.980
 And we've worked a lot with them the last five years to, again, piggyback on their successes and the fact that there's our potential customers are coming in and out each day.

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:41.620
 Just as I was reading up on the website, it seems like there's this there's an ethic of creativity and experimentation through the work that you do, where it's you're trying a lot of different things out to meet some of these needs or get at a more holistic or systemic piece of what's happening with hunger here.

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:58.320
 So, yeah, so and that's something I'm working on in the back end is how do we institutionalize the listening that we started off with to continue to have that be the core for our program development.

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:08.010
 And so a lot of our initiatives, we work with neighbors here at the farm and then we have to stay in communication and relationship with them.

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:11.610
 OK, you came out to do our health education course.

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:19.040
 What's the next the next hurdle that's keeping them from making long term lifestyle changes?

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:26.290
 And so from that, we had the play program stands for Petaluma Loves Active Youth.

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:35.190
 That was a partnership with the health center and that they had to stop coming out to the farm just due to logistics and cost.

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:41.290
 And so we took that initiative and then invited our neighbors to come to our farm to participate.

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:51.810
 But from there and when we're talking about, you know, what's what's next, what's needed, that's where we got the idea to do the food resource guide.

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:53.030
 So great.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:58.880
 We have this initiative to work with people for six weeks, but then we turn them out into the world.

00:09:58.880 --> 00:09:59.320
 Right.

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:03.430
 And it's up to them to figure out how do they sustain these behaviors?

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:12.400
 And so the food resource guide is a comprehensive list of where you can get free and reduced price food in Petaluma that helps make it that much easier.

00:10:12.670 --> 00:10:21.660
 So the next iteration or another iteration was we had often got requests from people that they wanted some sort of work trade.

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:26.620
 They want to they want to put sweat equity in to get access to fresh fruits and vegetables.

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:31.860
 And it's actually not legal, you know, because of labor laws, et cetera.

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:46.760
 And so what we did do is when we had the opportunity, we got a bilingual bounty hunters coordinator and really focused on recruiting volunteers from food pantry lines, physically able people.

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:53.740
 And that helped increase our volunteer numbers because there's more food out there than we can get to.

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:55.690
 We're limited by our volunteers.

00:10:56.110 --> 00:11:03.880
 And so we just shifted our initiative where people could take upwards of a third to a half of the produce that was harvested.

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:20.230
 And that's just a way of shifting that dynamic from perpetuating this situation where people have to wait in line for something that should be a right, which is fresh and healthy food and getting them to be part of the solution.

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:27.770
 I think at first glance, you could hear about Petaluma Bounty and think that it's a food access charity.

00:11:28.390 --> 00:11:32.490
 But it seems like so much of what you do is challenging what that is.

00:11:32.630 --> 00:11:34.990
 And you're meeting immediate need for food.

00:11:35.110 --> 00:11:39.390
 But at the same time, you're really challenging what some of the big problems that are with the idea that it's charity.

00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:39.670
 Right.

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:40.170
 Right.

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:40.710
 Exactly.

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:51.010
 We really and so that means how do we get more people on CalFresh or food stamps to utilize farmer's markets?

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:54.990
 And that's why we started the initiative called Farmer's Market Life.

00:11:55.310 --> 00:12:00.070
 It's a market match program for people in Sonoma County, basically.

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:09.070
 And if you're unfamiliar, it's for people to be able to get a dollar for dollar match for their produce using their CalFresh benefits.

00:12:09.650 --> 00:12:20.070
 And again, using that same idea, it's shifting away from giving things away to supporting multiple stakeholders of the food system.

00:12:20.170 --> 00:12:28.810
 So we're increasing low income folks purchasing power and we're increasing the customer base of small scale farmers.

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:39.460
 And the more we can align solutions and as our permaculture friends love to say, myself included, stacking functions, you know,

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:45.510
 we find solutions that benefit our environment, our local economy, our health, etc.

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:57.650
 And so that's where we try to push away from or just make it less one fix for all or considering moving away from only emergency food

00:12:57.650 --> 00:13:05.250
 or only a food bank system that is relying upon our conventional food system to work, to diversifying.

00:13:05.830 --> 00:13:06.800
 It's not replacing.

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:09.160
 I'm not advocating for that.

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:20.840
 But it's also talking to food banks and institutions that feed people, getting them to consider making incremental changes that could really support small and mid-sized farmers.

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:32.920
 Why does that need exist or why does a food bank, a more conventional foods bank function and where contradictions of that need to be complemented with more systemic thinking?

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:34.110
 Sure.

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:40.310
 So I'm not pointing the finger and saying anyone is wrong.

00:13:40.310 --> 00:13:50.400
 And so a good example is here in Sonoma County, over 90% of farm workers are food insecure.

00:13:51.930 --> 00:13:58.220
 And that's a staggering statistic that demonstrates that our food system is not working.

00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:06.620
 The people who are pouring their own sweat into it are not able to maintain a healthy lifestyle.

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:14.910
 So there are subsidies, you know, that exist in our food system, but they're just going to the wrong things.

00:14:15.710 --> 00:14:22.690
 And that's why it's so much easier and cheaper for people to purchase more processed food than fresh fruits and vegetables.

00:14:22.690 --> 00:14:34.950
 As it relates to food banks, I'm not the expert on this, but just from what I've seen, most food banks rely upon seconds and imperfects and unsold food.

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:53.470
 We're lucky here because we have the Central Valley, and I believe it's called Second Harvest, does a lot of harvesting or they purchase at pennies to the dollar the food that may have gone to waste or isn't economically viable for the farms to sell.

00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:57.530
 And then they reroute that into our food banks.

00:14:57.960 --> 00:15:05.670
 But the problem is the demand for food continues to increase at our food banks.

00:15:05.930 --> 00:15:13.910
 And so they're in this predicament where they need to find more and more cheap food in order to meet the growing needs.

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:24.230
 And so you can see by design, the better they do at their job, the actually worse off a lot of farm workers are.

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:28.520
 And so it's talking about diversifying the sources.

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:41.820
 I mean, you bring in you can talk about food waste and greenhouse gas emissions that some statistics say upwards of 40 percent of our food that's grown is wasted at some point, the process.

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:52.520
 And if we as a community worked better together to prevent that food going to waste and redirecting it to people who needed it, that's one solution.

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:56.180
 Another solution is subsidizing at the end user.

00:15:56.180 --> 00:16:04.450
 So talking about the CalFresh incentives as opposed to incentivizing large scale farms to grow more commodity crops.

00:16:06.530 --> 00:16:13.150
 You know, so these are just different ways of coming to solutions that help the consumers and the farms.

00:16:14.060 --> 00:16:20.600
 And I don't I don't I don't think we have all the answers, but we look at what's the greatest need.

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:28.100
 What are the problems that are really staring us in the face and what resources do we have as a community to respond to that?

00:16:29.290 --> 00:16:51.780
 And if you step out of kind of that linear thinking of we are one service provider and we the economy of scale thing, if we keep growing and providing that that one service, we might get more efficient at that at the loss of other opportunities or it might have greater costs long term.

00:16:52.940 --> 00:17:01.230
 So, yeah. So that makes it make a little more sense that there's so many different programs starting all the time and kind of some of them stopping.

00:17:01.430 --> 00:17:09.350
 But it's like there's a there's a cycle of reflection and creating new approaches and and then reflecting on those.

00:17:10.110 --> 00:17:19.450
 Yeah. And we're trying to put more intentionality behind that cycle of reflection and also more intention around, OK, we're going to step back now.

00:17:19.450 --> 00:17:25.740
 We feel like that initiative has it, you know, it's ready to take its training wheels off.

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:27.180
 It's ready to support itself.

00:17:27.580 --> 00:17:37.990
 There are a lot of people that wanted to see Petaluma Bounty just focus on community gardens and there would have been benefits the the to doing that.

00:17:39.670 --> 00:17:46.460
 The language around it would be a lot more simple and people would understand a lot more quickly what they were supporting.

00:17:47.040 --> 00:18:01.540
 But to me, then we would have had to expand our regional base and then you lose that personal contact and that nugget of innovation and creativity when you're truly in relationship with your community.

00:18:01.980 --> 00:18:06.860
 Maybe we can talk a little bit about the special context of here in Sonoma County and Petaluma.

00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:09.420
 It's a I mean, it's close to where I live.

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:11.040
 People listen all over the world.

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:12.480
 So is it a special case?

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:16.500
 It's such a productive place and it's famous for its food.

00:18:17.220 --> 00:18:24.680
 And yeah, I guess I was a little surprised to hear that the 90 percent of people working in farming farm workers were food insecure.

00:18:25.940 --> 00:18:29.460
 That was done by the Sonoma County Health Department last year.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:30.480
 That study.

00:18:31.480 --> 00:18:35.110
 And yeah, I mean, that's that's the thing.

00:18:35.270 --> 00:18:43.640
 And and that's also where we need to engage people in conversation about needs exist in your backyard.

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:54.230
 You know, instead of looking at other countries where there's poverty and and there's starvation, there's signs of malnutrition in your own community.

00:18:54.230 --> 00:18:59.530
 Right. And I'm part of a group of folks that meet monthly called the Hunger Index.

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:05.870
 And it's just one way of trying to measure the unmet need in our community.

00:19:06.250 --> 00:19:07.670
 I may have to edit these numbers.

00:19:07.810 --> 00:19:09.570
 Sorry, they're not at the top of my head.

00:19:09.570 --> 00:19:24.590
 But basically, given the higher cost of living in our community, it's an estimate of the number of households that make fifty five thousand dollars or less are considered to be potentially food insecure.

00:19:25.010 --> 00:19:36.810
 And so given the statistics on on the average cost for a healthy meal, it's measured that thirty four million meals go missing in Sonoma County alone.

00:19:36.810 --> 00:19:39.650
 People skipping meals because they don't have access to.

00:19:40.060 --> 00:19:40.500
 Exactly.

00:19:41.400 --> 00:19:52.780
 Or our seniors or people that are shut in, they only get the one meal a day through Meals on Wheels or everything else because hunger and food insecurity is very hard to measure.

00:19:53.200 --> 00:20:00.730
 Most people only rely upon the number of people seeking services, but there's a much greater need out there.

00:20:00.730 --> 00:20:05.110
 So this is just one attempt at trying to put some figures behind it.

00:20:05.510 --> 00:20:08.190
 And this is in a very food abundant community.

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:10.420
 It's similar to farmers markets.

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:28.190
 In 2013, when we were applying for this Feeney grant from the USDA, I was shocked to find out that each month, $5.4 million comes into our county through CalFresh, which is also known as SNAP, Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program.

00:20:28.190 --> 00:20:39.850
 And of that $5.4 million at the peak of the farmers market in 2013, about $4,000 went to, was used at farmers markets.

00:20:40.230 --> 00:20:44.720
 And so the rest of that's going to grocery stores and big box stores, right?

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:51.430
 And so there's a real opportunity there for our community to come together.

00:20:51.430 --> 00:20:54.930
 And buying at farmers market doesn't work for everyone.

00:20:55.410 --> 00:21:13.610
 But if we could get even a small percentage of our community, and in this instance, the CalFresh community, CalFresh customers, to consider buying a portion of their produce at farmers market, that could have a huge impact on the economic well-being of our farmers.

00:21:14.970 --> 00:21:22.220
 And so that's why we brought in this incentive program to acknowledge that food at farmers markets is more expensive.

00:21:22.490 --> 00:21:36.230
 Food, produce that's grown small scale, takes more labor, especially if you're not relying upon pesticides and a lot of mechanical cultivation.

00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:37.910
 It's just going to cost more.

00:21:38.950 --> 00:21:45.250
 We can talk about the economics, why it actually doesn't cost more if you start to add all the externalized costs.

00:21:45.750 --> 00:21:47.870
 But it does cost more.

00:21:48.050 --> 00:21:56.860
 And as in relationship with our community, we had to respond to that to say, OK, yeah, how can you pay more?

00:21:57.400 --> 00:22:05.390
 So we set up the incentive program and we're at that next layer of conversation because it's not as well utilized as it could or should be.

00:22:05.850 --> 00:22:09.460
 We don't use all of the incentives that we have.

00:22:09.460 --> 00:22:25.700
 So then we started a farmer's market tour initiative with the Center for Well-Being, bilingual, bicultural, promotores de salud that are bringing community members to the farmer's markets and introducing them to that concept.

00:22:26.340 --> 00:22:29.660
 These are people that are already coming to nutrition education classes.

00:22:29.900 --> 00:22:32.870
 So we're just making that extra connection.

00:22:33.150 --> 00:22:35.390
 Let's talk about community food security a little bit.

00:22:35.870 --> 00:22:41.110
 Chelsea and I, we ran a big research project in Mexico and in southern Mexico.

00:22:41.470 --> 00:22:48.730
 And we're working with the concept of food security and food sovereignty a lot, but at a household level.

00:22:48.730 --> 00:22:56.400
 So can you talk a little bit about what community food security is and how it's different from just individual or household food security?

00:22:56.900 --> 00:23:00.340
 Yeah, and I believe, and I don't have it memorized.

00:23:00.520 --> 00:23:08.350
 I'm sure there's a USDA definition and then there will be like a common person, you know, like myself definition.

00:23:09.370 --> 00:23:12.740
 And each household probably could define it differently.

00:23:12.980 --> 00:23:13.160
 Right.

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:22.590
 So community food security here and now, what it means to us is that more of our food is sourced from our community

00:23:22.750 --> 00:23:37.470
 and that more people feel like they can either through their own purchasing abilities or growing abilities or reaching out to some of the services that are provided,

00:23:38.760 --> 00:23:46.560
 that people are able to have regular, consistent access to healthy food that's culturally appropriate.

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:53.090
 So that takes a number of different approaches or we take a number of different approaches toward it.

00:23:53.270 --> 00:24:01.030
 And so the difference between household and community would be that there's a lot of community members that live in apartments, right?

00:24:01.400 --> 00:24:13.550
 And they may not be able to make ends meet, but how can we as a community come together to make sure they still have regular access to healthy food?

00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:17.840
 And that's where the community garden initiative came out of.

00:24:17.840 --> 00:24:22.100
 And we strategically built gardens near apartment buildings.

00:24:22.900 --> 00:24:28.550
 You know, so there's all these different ways of doing it, but saying that we're all in it together, you know,

00:24:28.590 --> 00:24:35.070
 as opposed to really focusing on the individual, being able to provide everything for themselves.

00:24:35.070 --> 00:24:46.240
 And perhaps in comparison to your experience, there's not a lot of, and this might please correct me and I'd love to learn more about the project,

00:24:46.820 --> 00:25:01.950
 but I'm not looking to push people to change their lifestyle so that it's focused on just being able to meet their needs as far as having a great garden or having a homestead.

00:25:01.950 --> 00:25:10.350
 And I think those are beautiful things, but it's a pretty privileged existence to be able to do that, especially in Sonoma County.

00:25:10.510 --> 00:25:16.690
 And when you look at food policy and worldwide, or I should say also free trade agreements,

00:25:17.230 --> 00:25:26.650
 a lot of our economic policies have undermined small-scale farmers in other countries to be able to exist or make a living doing what they're doing.

00:25:26.810 --> 00:25:28.570
 And so they had to shift away from that.

00:25:28.750 --> 00:25:33.310
 And that probably fit more into their lifestyle, community, and culture.

00:25:33.310 --> 00:25:39.720
 And so the community resilience might look very different there than it does here right now,

00:25:40.260 --> 00:25:48.760
 given, you know, the monocropping of grapes here and the fact that it costs so much to live here.

00:25:49.100 --> 00:25:54.560
 How we can come together, it's kind of like the compost conundrum here in Sonoma County too,

00:25:54.560 --> 00:26:04.110
 which is that our local compost facility was closed due to a very long political conflict.

00:26:04.650 --> 00:26:11.190
 So now our organic waste is being shipped out and people are being encouraged to do your own composting in your backyard.

00:26:11.190 --> 00:26:18.650
 And I believe that there was benefit to the model of having a centralized, larger-scale compost facility that was community-oriented,

00:26:19.490 --> 00:26:21.910
 you know, and that really supported nonprofits, etc.

00:26:22.150 --> 00:26:28.130
 So there are certain things that it's better to have a shared and centralized support system.

00:26:28.770 --> 00:26:33.730
 So we're kind of taking that approach with community food security,

00:26:33.890 --> 00:26:41.300
 but also trying to build the capacity of the individuals so that you can find ways to feed your family.

00:26:41.300 --> 00:26:45.740
 It just might not be you growing all your own food.

00:26:46.880 --> 00:26:52.210
 Another, if I could jump back, one thing I wanted to touch on with farmers markets.

00:26:52.350 --> 00:27:03.500
 One of the biggest barriers we have to getting more people on CalFresh to use farmers markets is because it's seen as so elitist.

00:27:04.730 --> 00:27:09.040
 And that foodie culture really works toward a disadvantage.

00:27:09.940 --> 00:27:16.500
 And people don't feel like farmers markets are very inviting spaces because it's been advertised.

00:27:16.960 --> 00:27:27.930
 You know, a lot of tourist money actually goes into talking about how our farmers markets are where chefs purchase their food and foodies and etc.

00:27:27.930 --> 00:27:36.330
 And so a lot of our community members don't feel comfortable going there, especially if they don't speak English as a primary language.

00:27:37.050 --> 00:27:45.910
 And so it's really fascinating how we're trying to develop together initiatives that will make our farmers markets more inviting to everyone.

00:27:46.050 --> 00:28:06.070
 I think that really gets at one of these key questions that keeps coming up this season about the California food movement is that California is this place over the last 50 years, especially that's been the center of this of really great local food and also the center of a lot of aspects of food justice and food sovereignty movements.

00:28:06.170 --> 00:28:11.980
 And sometimes they're totally in conflict and sometimes they feed off of each other in these really creative ways.

00:28:11.980 --> 00:28:18.700
 Yeah, so how do you start to address that cultural and economic kind of conundrum?

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:20.060
 Yeah, exactly.

00:28:20.480 --> 00:28:30.500
 Well, I mean, if you look at the average household income and what people are spending money on, the percentage going toward food has gone down significantly.

00:28:31.590 --> 00:28:43.060
 And so, I mean, I guess I'd make the radical statement that so much of our work is to revalue our food, you know, to get people to really be aware of what it takes to grow food well.

00:28:43.790 --> 00:28:52.910
 And that food should cost more, but we need to work together as a community to make sure that doesn't keep people that have limited resources to be able to participate.

00:28:53.520 --> 00:28:55.780
 And that's really the crux of what we're doing here.

00:28:56.280 --> 00:28:58.060
 High quality food should cost more.

00:28:58.810 --> 00:28:59.690
 It's a higher value.

00:28:59.830 --> 00:29:01.230
 There's higher nutrient density.

00:29:01.870 --> 00:29:05.730
 It costs farmers more to make it in the way that we love to eat it.

00:29:06.370 --> 00:29:17.600
 So that's where the subsidies coming in at the end make a lot more sense because it maintains the value of the food where the farmer can get closer to making a living wage.

00:29:18.480 --> 00:29:19.360
 Not there yet.

00:29:19.360 --> 00:29:23.910
 And it still allows low-income folks to participate, you know.

00:29:23.970 --> 00:29:25.730
 So that's one example.

00:29:26.090 --> 00:29:28.710
 We need to collectively start thinking of another example.

00:29:28.870 --> 00:29:32.550
 It's also because we don't know if the funding will exist in a couple of years.

00:29:33.120 --> 00:29:39.130
 So I support this idea that our food should be more expensive.

00:29:40.190 --> 00:29:48.570
 You know, if you look at everything put into it, well, it's the fact that petroleum is subsidized, you know, and everything else to get it to be this cheap.

00:29:48.810 --> 00:29:53.370
 So what are different ways that we can work together to make it work better for everyone?

00:29:53.370 --> 00:30:03.890
 And then, you know, you bring in the conversation about food waste and, well, if you bought less but used more of it, that's a you, me, whomever, right?

00:30:04.570 --> 00:30:10.710
 And got more out of the buying higher quality food, you actually might save money.

00:30:11.070 --> 00:30:14.130
 So that's just another approach in conversation.

00:30:14.130 --> 00:30:23.760
 And so it starts with getting people excited, learn where their food comes from, and to kickstart their imagination and relationship, building a better relationship to food.

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:36.220
 And then taking on conversations about why organic costs more, why it should cost more, and what we're doing together to make that still work for everyone.

00:30:36.880 --> 00:30:42.220
 Speaking of food waste, I want to talk about gleaning before I ever get to go there.

00:30:42.360 --> 00:30:48.870
 But yeah, is that where you started after the farm and kind of delving into a bigger food system here?

00:30:49.310 --> 00:30:51.150
 Me personally, or our program?

00:30:51.150 --> 00:30:52.110
 The program, yeah.

00:30:52.430 --> 00:30:57.220
 So gleaning came on alongside the community gardens.

00:30:57.420 --> 00:30:59.280
 And so it's been going on for quite some time.

00:30:59.680 --> 00:31:05.240
 Something that is really fascinating is it complements existing networks.

00:31:06.200 --> 00:31:12.440
 And it actually brings the price down for these local pantries that have to purchase food from our food banks, right?

00:31:12.890 --> 00:31:16.520
 And everyone's really pleased with the quality of the food that we bring.

00:31:16.520 --> 00:31:25.320
 In a high-functioning year, when we have a paid coordinator, we would normally bring in around 120,000 to 130,000 pounds of food.

00:31:25.520 --> 00:31:32.830
 When it's a volunteer-based initiative, like whomever steps up to take on that role, we're always grateful.

00:31:33.250 --> 00:31:35.130
 And there isn't as much consistency.

00:31:36.010 --> 00:31:38.750
 That number drops to about 5,000 pounds per year.

00:31:38.750 --> 00:31:44.560
 And so that just goes to show you, well, number one, that we need to be able to provide a consistent service.

00:31:44.820 --> 00:31:52.900
 And number two, that we, like everyone else, need support doing the unsexy things of coordinating volunteers, right?

00:31:53.000 --> 00:32:00.080
 And without those key positions, these networks start to kind of fall in upon themselves, yeah.

00:32:00.260 --> 00:32:01.540
 So how does gleaning work?

00:32:01.640 --> 00:32:08.580
 It is volunteers who go pick fruit or where do you find this extra fruit and how does that all work?

00:32:08.580 --> 00:32:15.940
 Yeah, and so we collect fruits and vegetables and extra food from farmers markets.

00:32:16.540 --> 00:32:25.220
 We have now organized drop sites at local pantries to help direct gardeners and other people that have extra food to deliver it directly.

00:32:25.880 --> 00:32:34.000
 And then we have a group of volunteers that, when called upon, will go out and harvest food if the homeowner can't themselves.

00:32:34.350 --> 00:32:49.040
 And so we have a volunteer coordinator and we do regular trainings here at our farm on appropriate ways to be on a farm, to not stop on plants, etc., how to harvest all the different produce.

00:32:49.760 --> 00:32:57.120
 And then we'll meet here like on a Saturday and then carpool over to the site, harvest what's available or what we have time to get.

00:32:57.120 --> 00:33:03.070
 And then take it to either a pantry or cot's kitchen, which is our local homeless shelter.

00:33:03.570 --> 00:33:05.790
 So that's, yeah, generally what it looks like.

00:33:05.970 --> 00:33:10.430
 I read on the website that you harvested over a half million pounds of food that way.

00:33:10.670 --> 00:33:14.050
 There must just be so much food that doesn't make it to market.

00:33:14.370 --> 00:33:14.690
 Definitely.

00:33:15.050 --> 00:33:19.630
 And we aren't able to respond to all the phone calls that we get either.

00:33:19.930 --> 00:33:21.330
 And so it's heartbreaking.

00:33:21.650 --> 00:33:39.040
 I honestly think that's one of the, if there are reasons for burnout for volunteers and the volunteer coordinator, it's seeing all the things that we can't get to and that sense of loss, knowing that it could be so much appreciated and other, a couple blocks away sometimes, right?

00:33:39.480 --> 00:33:44.960
 So a lot of that food actually also is included in our driver's network.

00:33:44.960 --> 00:33:52.710
 And so it's not just harvested, it's harvested, recovered and redistributed.

00:33:53.180 --> 00:33:55.160
 Yeah, it's kind of like the whole food bank thing.

00:33:55.560 --> 00:33:59.520
 Can anyone put a measure on the amount of food that's going into the waste, right?

00:33:59.540 --> 00:34:03.330
 Like if a tree falls in the forest, does it make us out?

00:34:03.470 --> 00:34:03.710
 Right.

00:34:03.810 --> 00:34:07.150
 So we don't even know how much is truly out there.

00:34:08.010 --> 00:34:10.830
 Suzy, do you see yourself as part of a food movement?

00:34:11.490 --> 00:34:11.650
 Yeah.

00:34:12.350 --> 00:34:12.860
 Totally.

00:34:14.210 --> 00:34:14.630
 Yeah.

00:34:15.140 --> 00:34:29.580
 Um, I'm not very hip though, you know, like, um, I live in and breathe this stuff and then I often don't have time to catch up with other people doing more exciting and innovating things.

00:34:30.040 --> 00:34:36.590
 Um, and so I'm as much part of the movement as, um, the movement's willing to have me.

00:34:37.710 --> 00:34:42.750
 So many people have responded that way to this question that I've interviewed this season.

00:34:43.010 --> 00:34:55.370
 And, um, I think part of the reason that we started this show was, uh, feeling like definitely this is what we do as part of this movement, but also never, not always feeling part of a food movement.

00:34:55.650 --> 00:34:55.890
 Yeah.

00:34:56.010 --> 00:35:02.990
 I think there's so much value in connecting and hearing and also commiserating sometimes, right?

00:35:03.350 --> 00:35:06.090
 Like, um, this is really hard work.

00:35:06.310 --> 00:35:12.460
 And, um, I'm presently, I'm not out in the fields doing the work on the farm.

00:35:12.540 --> 00:35:16.500
 I'm, I'm more inside, um, which is its own journey.

00:35:16.500 --> 00:35:32.110
 And, you know, I feel like my solar batteries are, are in need of more charging being outside, but I'm trying to do my part to shift the food system because I see this being such a common thread and everything that we talk about.

00:35:32.110 --> 00:35:43.610
 And, um, it, um, it's part of why when I try to make more, uh, radical political change in my younger years, I got so fed up with institutions of power.

00:35:43.870 --> 00:35:51.800
 I just went back to community and started making relationships happen and building community around food.

00:35:51.880 --> 00:35:56.750
 And there's something very powerful and wholesome, uh, about that.

00:35:56.870 --> 00:36:00.960
 And then, so I feel like a lot of our work is taking it to the next level.

00:36:01.300 --> 00:36:13.540
 And, um, we all know that something feels right when we're here on the bounty farm, having a potluck, you know, and can we take what's right in essence and expand it and make sure everyone has opportunity to do the same.

00:36:13.660 --> 00:36:18.350
 It's a very safe place to start, um, these more political conversations.

00:36:18.650 --> 00:36:32.790
 But as it relates to the food movement, you know, I think also being the systems person, it's really unsexy, like, because, you know, there's a lot of capital going toward, um, right now for profit food recovery.

00:36:33.090 --> 00:36:36.590
 And I'm like the grumpy naysayer in the background.

00:36:36.590 --> 00:36:38.850
 That's like, how's that different than anything else?

00:36:38.930 --> 00:36:44.890
 You know, are you really innovating or did you just repackage the same idea, et cetera?

00:36:45.070 --> 00:36:51.230
 And so maybe I won't be invited to, like, new parties of new products, et cetera.

00:36:51.860 --> 00:36:54.520
 I, I'm not that drawn to the sparkly things.

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:59.480
 So that's where I feel like, I guess I'm part of the movement.

00:36:59.820 --> 00:37:13.970
 But I think the more you can get, uh, maybe those of us that are less social or less able to tell our own story beautifully and wrapped in a bow, the more you can get us hearing from each other.

00:37:14.310 --> 00:37:17.480
 I think that'll take innovation to a whole new level.

00:37:18.250 --> 00:37:27.570
 I guess if I were to, uh, identify my, my part in this, it would be, there is innovation, but it's also incremental change.

00:37:27.570 --> 00:37:42.070
 And I remember just reading an article about some of the most progressive change will come from paper pushers and, you know, administrators making small changes and how their agencies or departments are run.

00:37:42.470 --> 00:37:47.240
 And so, you know, there's small radical changes that we can all make in our daily lives.

00:37:47.360 --> 00:37:55.530
 You and Petaluma Bounty as a, as a organization, as, as a community, you're, you're always kind of, you're trying new approaches, experimenting with things.

00:37:55.650 --> 00:38:02.100
 Are there things that you're kind of dreaming about trying now that haven't started that you can talk about?

00:38:02.100 --> 00:38:08.490
 Well, we are so lucky to, to hire a community engagement coordinator and education.

00:38:08.490 --> 00:38:09.490
 She does both.

00:38:09.490 --> 00:38:22.770
 And our dream is to have an educator here on site to bring more community out here again, to take on the food literacy piece, to drive food purchasing and healthy behavior.

00:38:23.330 --> 00:38:32.980
 Um, so there's that, but then with our farmer's market life initiative, perhaps having an educator bring schools to the farmer's markets, right?

00:38:33.120 --> 00:38:50.860
 So I think it's really looking at the farm to school to farm movement, you know, and how a lot of us have forgotten that piece, but not in a agritourism sort of way, but in a, let's dive deep into this conversation together about community food security.

00:38:51.080 --> 00:38:54.920
 So taking education and engagement to the next level.

00:38:55.280 --> 00:38:59.460
 And then, uh, on this farm site, we're working out having a long-term lease.

00:39:00.380 --> 00:39:16.580
 And so we would love to take the building over there and turn it into, um, well, my dream is a dance hall, but also a community space and perhaps a commercial kitchen to support new initiatives and, you know, cottage food industry type things.

00:39:16.580 --> 00:39:20.710
 And then address some of the food waste that even happens here on our farm, right?

00:39:21.010 --> 00:39:22.710
 Um, to extend into the seasons.

00:39:23.280 --> 00:39:36.280
 Um, I think also if we could find a way to mentor other communities to be able to do their own multifaceted initiatives, that would be a dream of mine.

00:39:36.460 --> 00:39:40.520
 There's a lot of push right now for nonprofits to maximize program income.

00:39:40.520 --> 00:39:49.070
 And I'm trying to find the synergies and how to do that in ways that actually benefits our organization and doesn't pull us away from our mission.

00:39:49.190 --> 00:39:56.040
 And so if there were ways to mentor and or consult other communities, um, that might be something for us to look at.

00:39:56.360 --> 00:39:56.460
 Awesome.

00:39:56.540 --> 00:39:57.660
 Well, that's been great.

00:39:57.800 --> 00:40:03.100
 We did a ton of talking, but I would think we could probably even talk for another hour about some of these things.

00:40:03.460 --> 00:40:09.290
 Um, is there anything that, any last thing that you want to leave us with or something that I didn't ask about?

00:40:10.180 --> 00:40:10.500
 Hmm.

00:40:11.450 --> 00:40:15.100
 Well, I'll think of something, you know, five minutes after we drive away, of course.

00:40:15.400 --> 00:40:28.590
 But, um, I mean, I think right now, um, what's coming up a lot for me is how there's organizations in our community that take one piece of the problem.

00:40:28.590 --> 00:40:31.890
 Um, and then it's kind of their territory of sorts.

00:40:31.890 --> 00:40:35.310
 And there's a lot of turf wars that go on in nonprofit worlds.

00:40:35.810 --> 00:40:48.500
 I wish there was a way to open up conversation where we could talk about, oh, it looks like your logic model and our logic model or scope of work have some rubs, you know, like let's just pull it out and map it out.

00:40:48.500 --> 00:40:58.030
 And then talk proactively about how we could turn that potential natural competitive piece into collaboration.

00:40:59.050 --> 00:41:09.630
 And so I'm really excited to work with my staff on, on just talking about different approaches and internalizing the, um, community listening.

00:41:10.230 --> 00:41:15.730
 Also trying to, uh, read up on different organizational development models.

00:41:16.070 --> 00:41:19.100
 And there's a book called redefining organizations.

00:41:19.100 --> 00:41:27.100
 I'm really excited about to re democratize our own organization, to make it reflect our guiding principles inside and out.

00:41:27.440 --> 00:41:27.720
 So, yeah.

00:41:27.800 --> 00:41:28.100
 Oh, good.

00:41:28.160 --> 00:41:33.510
 I'm glad you said this because Katie, uh, Katie Hackmeyer, who is a good friend and who is on the board, right?

00:41:33.670 --> 00:41:33.990
 Yeah.

00:41:34.370 --> 00:41:37.430
 Uh, and it was also the first person we interviewed for this podcast.

00:41:37.430 --> 00:41:40.130
 She, um, she said to ask about exactly that.

00:41:40.270 --> 00:41:45.310
 The nonprofit world can not always be that conducive to, to movement building.

00:41:45.470 --> 00:41:50.230
 And you're really thinking a lot about how to kind of a nitty gritty of how to make it more about movement building.

00:41:50.570 --> 00:41:51.010
 Yeah.

00:41:51.010 --> 00:42:04.690
 Well, and it's, again, it, are you trying to solve one problem like hunger in the community or are you trying to develop the capacity of your community to be able to respond to that problem?

00:42:04.690 --> 00:42:09.010
 Um, that may mean us not being the expert in all, all fields.

00:42:09.010 --> 00:42:09.370
 Right.

00:42:10.150 --> 00:42:30.150
 And it also means to me, I mean, maybe in five years I'll have a different opinion, but I hear a lot of ego in marketing and I'm really uncomfortable about how I'm supposed to nudge past others and tell everyone that Petaluma Bounty has the answer for all.

00:42:30.150 --> 00:42:40.970
 Well, I'm really excited or I, I'm very honored to be part of this project and I, I believe in it, but I also believe that each community needs to map out their own way.

00:42:41.230 --> 00:42:51.040
 And so we're really trying to boil down what we're doing into here's an approach to apply to your community, but not to say that we have the answer.

00:42:51.430 --> 00:42:55.610
 Therefore, send us a check for $50 and we'll send you your toolkit.

00:42:55.610 --> 00:43:01.490
 It's, you know, and so there's authenticity in that, but there's also, it's not very sexy.

00:43:02.170 --> 00:43:07.330
 I'll tell people, yeah, I don't think we can help with that or, or something.

00:43:07.510 --> 00:43:14.190
 And, you know, uh, in the nonprofit world, there's a lot of, of selling that goes on selling of self.

00:43:14.400 --> 00:43:21.970
 And, um, it, I think it's in direct conflict with everyone's aspirations of being more collaborative.

00:43:22.150 --> 00:43:31.570
 And so I think most folks know me in our community meetings that I'll, I'll speak my truth, but I'm so honored to be in spaces where I can.

00:43:31.570 --> 00:43:48.520
 And then when I, I don't agree, or I see that you're, for instance, your approach is actually, um, hampering more than, than helping, but it's, it's a vulnerability that you don't hear very much in positions of leadership that I think is very lacking.

00:43:48.520 --> 00:43:55.950
 And given the complexity of our problems today, I don't think anyone has the answer.

00:43:55.950 --> 00:44:02.370
 I think the answer exists in community and diverse thinking and, um, crowdsourcing.

00:44:02.790 --> 00:44:07.990
 And so finding ways to harness that, that again, reflects democratic values.

00:44:08.110 --> 00:44:16.580
 Um, so I'm not, I'm not saying anything specific, but it's an ethic and it's a culture that I think needs to start.

00:44:17.060 --> 00:44:31.860
 And, you know, I think just like the CSA model and why it was so, in some communities, so amazing is that, uh, farmers would open their books and just say, this is what I need.

00:44:31.860 --> 00:44:35.730
 And, or, you know, I'm not, I'm not trying to make a killing here.

00:44:35.810 --> 00:44:36.630
 Right.

00:44:36.690 --> 00:44:39.290
 And so that might be something that we consider in the future.

00:44:40.020 --> 00:44:52.990
 Um, I'm pretty open about, you know, uh, our books and how much money we don't make here, but, um, you know, finding ways to build trust because, uh, we're also entering.

00:44:53.470 --> 00:45:06.640
 Well, we're already in an era where there's so little trust and, and large institutions because we've lost sight of what we were, a lot of our agencies were truly made for in the first place.

00:45:06.840 --> 00:45:20.540
 Oh man, I could ask you a million questions about this, but I guess one of the things that I think you're going to talk about it is that, um, maybe the world of food is such a good fertile place to, to start to redefine that.

00:45:20.540 --> 00:45:39.380
 Because I've heard that from farmers and, and especially in Latin America and the agroecology movement talking about like, there are no recipes for this thing we call agroecology is something that you invent and you work out in each place with, with each community, but it's a, it's an ethic and a, and a, and a approach to it.

00:45:39.380 --> 00:45:58.500
 Um, there's principles, but there's not a set of guidelines and I've heard it, um, I mean, it's, it's where, uh, diversity, whether it's biodiversity or it's kind of the diversity of people or kinds of thought really goes from becoming something that's just, um, something you're supposed to want to something that's essential for being able to adapt to change.

00:45:58.700 --> 00:46:07.070
 So, but there is that conflict with, with raising money and with being able to put your finger on what it is just exactly that you do.

00:46:07.170 --> 00:46:07.350
 Right.

00:46:07.690 --> 00:46:08.090
 Exactly.

00:46:09.160 --> 00:46:13.860
 Um, and you know, there used to be a nationwide community food security network.

00:46:13.860 --> 00:46:14.340
 Right.

00:46:14.690 --> 00:46:30.550
 Um, and I was so impressed with, with their work and, um, I know they no longer exist, but I think, um, I don't know the story behind that, but I imagine some of it too is just the, yeah, but what widget do you make at the end of the day?

00:46:30.690 --> 00:46:31.140
 Right.

00:46:31.800 --> 00:46:34.460
 And I need to get better at storytelling.

00:46:34.460 --> 00:46:38.780
 A lot of us do what story we're, we're, we're weaving.

00:46:39.220 --> 00:46:39.380
 Right.

00:46:40.160 --> 00:46:47.590
 Um, but I also don't want to hold space that I know the answers.

00:46:47.710 --> 00:47:01.260
 This came up in a recent conversation that might be where we look to incorporate more of the quote unquote feminine into leadership models where it's collaborative and where we're comfortable.

00:47:01.260 --> 00:47:12.550
 If a leader says, I don't know the answer, we need to do this together or, you know, not just that I'll get back to you, but like, this is, this is bigger than me.

00:47:12.550 --> 00:47:13.450
 Right.

00:47:13.530 --> 00:47:17.010
 And it also goes beyond linear binary thinking.

00:47:17.010 --> 00:47:26.700
 The fact that so many of our, our health problems and pollution has multi-point, um, potential causality.

00:47:26.700 --> 00:47:38.950
 Like it, it's not that our problems are caused by one thing that we just need to recalibrate and our systems will work or we'll be healthier or it'll solve cancer.

00:47:39.110 --> 00:47:43.290
 Sometimes, you know, sometimes yes, but a lot of times no.

00:47:43.290 --> 00:47:46.230
 And so we need diverse mindsets.

00:47:46.230 --> 00:47:52.870
 We need people that are willing to step out to of the scientific method.

00:47:53.430 --> 00:48:14.100
 And, you know, that we have blind spots and I don't know about you, but part of what aligned me with this work was I could project problems happening because I see patterns often where, where other people don't, or they get so excited by, you know, that new way of packaging an old solution.

00:48:14.420 --> 00:48:19.930
 And yeah, it's just, it's very clear that we need other approaches.

00:48:19.990 --> 00:48:28.390
 And so that's, I'm still learning so much, but the idea of service leadership comes into play.

00:48:28.690 --> 00:48:34.250
 But yeah, no, it's looking at how do I, not spending too much time belly button gazing, right?

00:48:34.890 --> 00:48:45.600
 But at the same time, what role do I want to play in remaking food movement, my own organization, you know, that I'm a part of, et cetera.

00:48:46.540 --> 00:48:47.020
 Awesome.

00:48:47.020 --> 00:48:48.480
 Well, thanks so much, Susie.

00:48:48.600 --> 00:48:49.740
 It's been totally great.

